 ## Overview
-Specific Model Architectures refers to the family of neural network structures and representation schemes used to parameterize the radiance field function $f_\theta(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{d})$ in differentiable volume rendering. While the standard NeRF [Parent] uses a single MLP to map 3D coordinates and view directions to density and color, this family encompasses modifications to that core mapping to address specific limitations such as training speed, generalization, geometric ambiguity, memory efficiency, and dynamic scene modeling. These architectures differ from their siblings in the [NeRF Training Loop](970-nerf-training-loop.md) by defining *what* is being optimized (the network structure and data representation) rather than *how* it is optimized (the sampling strategies, loss functions, or loop configurations).
