 ## Background
-The original NeRF paper [S2] introduced the basic MLP architecture. Subsequent research has rapidly expanded this into a diverse family of architectures addressing specific challenges such as generalization [S1, unofficial], efficiency [S1, unofficial], geometry [S3], and dynamics [S5].
+The original NeRF architecture, introduced by Mildenhall et al. in 2020, established the baseline MLP-based radiance field representation [S2]. Subsequent developments have focused on improving rendering speed (e.g., AdaNeRF, Neural Light Fields), handling dynamic scenes (e.g., Deformable Radiance Fields), enabling single-view synthesis (e.g., NeRDi, pi-GAN), and improving geometric fidelity (e.g., Geometric Priors, IRON). The field has expanded to include generative models, sparse representations, and hybrid architectures that integrate CNNs, Transformers, and diffusion models.
 
